    <article style="border-top: 1px solid #e2e8f0; padding-top: 3.5rem; line-height: 1.85; color: #334155;">
      
      <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3.5vw, 1.85rem); font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-bottom: 1rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; line-height: 1.3;">
        Mathematical Principles of Continuous Fixed-Bed Adsorption &amp; Gas Separation
      </h2>
      <p>
        While batch adsorption isotherm modeling describes thermodynamic equilibrium saturation in a closed vessel, real-world chemical separation units (such as industrial water treatment filters, Pressure Swing Adsorption (PSA) columns, and carbon capture beds) operate under continuous dynamic flow. As the fluid phase flows through the packed porous sorbent, a transient concentration wave known as the <strong>Mass Transfer Zone (MTZ)</strong> moves through the column.
      </p>

      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        1. The Thomas Breakthrough Model
      </h3>
      <p>
        The Thomas model is one of the most widely applied analytical expressions for describing fixed-bed column breakthrough dynamics. It presumes plug-flow hydrodynamics with negligible axial dispersion and second-order Langmuir kinetics:
      </p>
      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border-left: 4px solid #0d9488; padding: 1rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 0.95rem; overflow-x: auto; line-height: 1.6;">
        <span>C_t / C_0 = 1 / [ 1 + exp( (k_Th * q_0 * m / Q) - k_Th * C_0 * t ) ]</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Where <em>C_t</em> is effluent concentration at time <em>t</em>, <em>C_0</em> is inlet concentration, <em>k_Th</em> is the Thomas rate constant (mL/(min·mg)), <em>q_0</em> is the maximum solid uptake capacity (mg/g), <em>m</em> is adsorbent mass, and <em>Q</em> is volumetric flow rate.
      </p>

      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        2. The Bohart-Adams Model (Surface Reaction Kinetics)
      </h3>
      <p>
        Formulated for describing fixed-bed chlorine and gas breakthrough, the Bohart-Adams model applies to the initial region of the breakthrough curve (<em>C_t / C_0 &lt; 0.15</em>):
      </p>
      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border-left: 4px solid #0d9488; padding: 1rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 0.95rem; overflow-x: auto; line-height: 1.6;">
        <span>ln(C_t / C_0) = k_BA · C_0 · t - (k_BA · N_0 · Z) / u</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Where <em>k_BA</em> is the kinetic constant (L/(mg·min)), <em>N_0</em> is the volumetric saturation capacity (mg/L or mg/cm³), <em>Z</em> is the bed depth (cm), and <em>u</em> is linear superficial fluid velocity (cm/min).
      </p>

      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        3. Mass Transfer Zone (MTZ) &amp; Bed Efficiency
      </h3>
      <p>
        The dynamic performance of an adsorption column is governed by the length of the Mass Transfer Zone (MTZ). The breakthrough time (<em>t_b</em>) is typically designated when effluent leakage reaches 5% (<em>C_t/C_0 = 0.05</em>), while saturation (<em>t_s</em>) occurs at 95% (<em>C_t/C_0 = 0.95</em>):
      </p>
      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border-left: 4px solid #0d9488; padding: 1rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 0.95rem; overflow-x: auto; line-height: 1.6;">
        <span>L_MTZ = Z · [ (t_s - t_b) / t_s ]</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        A narrow MTZ indicates rapid intraparticle diffusion and minimal mass transfer resistance, maximizing the fraction of the bed utilized before breakthrough occurs.
      </p>

      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        4. Binary Gas Separation Selectivity in PSA/VPSA
      </h3>
      <p>
        In Pressure Swing Adsorption (PSA) processes for carbon capture or biogas upgrading, the competitive adsorption selectivity (&alpha;<sub>A/B</sub>) quantifies the affinity of component A over component B:
      </p>
      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border-left: 4px solid #0d9488; padding: 1rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 0.95rem; overflow-x: auto; line-height: 1.6;">
        <span>α_A/B = (q_A / y_A) / (q_B / y_B)</span>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ Accordion -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Frequently Asked Questions: Adsorption Column Dynamics &amp; Gas Separation
      </h3>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What is the difference between breakthrough time and exhaustion time?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Breakthrough time (t_b) is the point at which the effluent adsorbate concentration reaches an allowable limit (typically 5% of feed concentration, Ct/C0 = 0.05). Exhaustion time (t_s) is when the bed is nearly saturated (typically Ct/C0 = 0.95).
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How does column bed depth affect breakthrough time?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Increasing bed depth (Z) proportionally increases the sorbent mass and fluid residence time (Empty Bed Contact Time, EBCT). This extends breakthrough time and gives the mass transfer zone more distance to fully develop.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Why is pressure drop crucial in Pressure Swing Adsorption (PSA)?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Excessive pressure drop across packed pellet beds (calculated via the Ergun equation) creates parasitic energy losses in feed compressors and vacuum pumps, while high superficial gas velocities risk fluidizing or crushing adsorbent pellets.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

    </article>

